<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indian Wild Ass Sanctuary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1972</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indian Wild Ass Sanctuary is a protected area in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-rann-of-kutch">Little Rann of Kutch</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, established in 1972. Covering nearly 5,000 square kilometres of salt flats, grassland and seasonal wetland, it is the last refuge of the Indian wild ass, or khur, whose numbers have recovered from a few hundred in the 1960s to several thousand. It also shelters large numbers of migratory birds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indian-wild-ass-sanctuary.jpg" alt="Indian Wild Ass Sanctuary" width="320" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Asim Patel · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IndianWildAss_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
